            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "pattern correlation",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The 1979-2018 linear trend in annual mean temperature in ERA5 is shown in Fig. 2a. The reanalysis indicates a global mean warming of 0.7 \u00b0C during this period, but with local trends ranging from a cooling of 2 \u00b0C (off the coast of East Antarctica) to a warming of 7 \u00b0C (north of Novaya Zemlya). The distribution of trends in ERA5 has a high pattern correlation (r=0.81) with the CMIP5 multi-model mean (Fig. 2b).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "explained variance",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Figure 1 quantifies the performance of the trajectory-based regression by the explained variance E = 1 \u2212 \u0394T2 res 1979\u22122018 \u0394T2 ERA5 1979\u22122018 (1) where \u2206Tres is the difference between the analyzed (\u2206TERA5) and predicted temperature anomalies, and both of these have been linearly detrended over 1979\u20132018 to exclude long-term climate change.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ERA5",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "mean square difference",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The residual monthly mean temperature trends also tend to agree better with the multi-model mean temperature trends from models in the 5th Coupled Model Intercomparison Project (CMIP5) than the original ERA5 trends do, with a 42% decrease in the mean square difference over the global land area. However, the corresponding decrease in the mean square difference of the annual mean temperature trends is only 6%.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
